\description{
Generates a synthetic two-image forced-choice (2IFC) dataset that
is shape-compatible with every \verb{check_*()}, \code{run_diagnostics()},
\code{ci_from_responses_2ifc()}, and reliability/discriminability
function in the package. Useful as a quickstart sandbox and as a
building block for simulation studies (power, calibration of
reliability and discriminability metrics, sensitivity to
contamination).

The function generates the noise pool on the fly via
\code{\link[rcicr:generateNoisePattern]{rcicr::generateNoisePattern()}} and \code{\link[rcicr:generateNoiseImage]{rcicr::generateNoiseImage()}},
so it requires the \code{rcicr} package to be installed. With default
arguments (50 participants per condition, 500 trials, 256-pixel
images) the noise generation step takes one to a few minutes; a
progress bar is shown.
}
\details{
When \code{rdata_dir = NULL}, the returned \verb{$rdata_path} points at a
session tempdir and becomes invalid after the R session ends.
Persist the simulation across sessions (caching with
\code{\link[=saveRDS]{saveRDS()}}, knitr \code{cache=TRUE}, sharing with collaborators)
either by passing an explicit \code{rdata_dir} such as \code{"simdata/"},
or by handing the returned \verb{$stimuli} list to
\code{\link[=ci_from_responses_2ifc]{ci_from_responses_2ifc()}} (and the other infoval-dependent
helpers) in place of \code{rdata_path}. \verb{$stimuli} is a
self-contained in-memory representation of the rcicr stimuli
env, so the sim object round-trips through
\code{saveRDS()}/\code{readRDS()} without a file dependency.
}
\section{Signal model}{

Each trial \code{t} shows two stimuli, image_a = base + noise\link{t} and
image_b = base - noise\link{t}. The participant chooses one. With
\code{signal_strength = "none"} choices are uniform random (\code{P(+1) = 0.5}); with \code{"weak"} / \code{"strong"} (or a custom numeric \code{beta}),
the log-odds of choosing image_a are
\code{beta * (noise[, t] \%*\% s) / scale}, where \code{s} is a binary mask
from \code{\link[=make_face_mask]{make_face_mask()}} over the requested \code{signal_region} and
\code{scale = sqrt(sum(s))} keeps the per-pixel signal magnitude
comparable across regions of different size.
}

\section{RT model}{

Shifted lognormal: \code{rt = round(exp(rnorm(n, log(800), 0.5)) + 150)}, in ms. A small fraction of fast (<200 ms) and slow
(>5000 ms) contaminants are mixed in (default 2\% each) so the
diagnostic functions (\code{\link[=check_rt]{check_rt()}}) have something to flag.
}

